- InOut: item (DINT), buffer (ARRAY[1..4] OF DINT), capacity (DINT) is input (so it's an input, not inout). The buffer is INOUT because we need to both read and write.

We need to implement a circular queue with indices head and tail. The typical approach: head points to the index of the first element (the front), tail points to the next free slot (the position where next element will be enqueued). elementCount holds number of valid elements. Buffer is used as circular storage.

We need to implement rising edge detection for enqueue and dequeue. The function block should be stateless across cycles, but need internal state: headIdx (position of front), tailIdx (position of next empty), elementCount. We also need previous values for enqueue and dequeue to detect rising edges.

